# Pull remote before changes

Before creating, editing, or deleting any project file, pull the current branch from its remote tracking branch. Do this in every repo that will be changed.

## Required sequence

1. Identify every git repo that will be changed.
2. Run `git status -sb` in that repo.
3. Run `git pull` against the current branch's upstream.
4. Apply changes only after the pull succeeds.

## Stop instead of changing

- The pull fails (conflicts, rejected update, no upstream, or a network error).
- Uncommitted local work would be overwritten or would block the pull.
- The branch has diverged and a merge or rebase needs a decision.

Do not stash, reset, discard, or force-update to make the pull succeed unless the user explicitly asks.

Read-only work (questions, search, review) does not require a pull.
